<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franconia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franconia is a historical region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> centred on the valley of the Main, now mostly in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> with parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. One of the stem duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, it was dominated in the early 10th century by the Conradines, whose leader became King Conrad I. Later divided among prince-bishoprics, Hohenzollern margraves, counts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a>, it formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franconia.jpg" alt="Franconia" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikmaq · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franconia_(Germany).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
